   "section": "faq",
   "items": [
    {
     "role": "heading",
     "order": 0,
     "text": "Frequently Asked Questions"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 1,
     "text": "What is the difference between an HDD and SSD?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 2,
     "text": "HDDs (hard disk drives) store data on spinning magnetic platters and deliver the highest capacities available — often at a fraction of the cost per terabyte of flash storage. They're the standard for , backup, surveillance, content archives, and enterprise data centers, where capacity, reliability, and cost-per-terabyte matter most. SSDs use flash memory and offer faster read/write speeds, suited to operating systems and active applications. Most storage setups use HDDs for bulk capacity and flash only where speed is critical."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 3,
     "text": "NAS",
     "href": "/solutions/network-attached-storage/nas-hard-drives"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 4,
     "text": "How much storage do I need?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 5,
     "text": "Personal users typically need 1–4 TB. Creative professionals working with 4K video usually need 8–20 TB. Small businesses generally start at 10–40 TB in configurations, and workloads scale into petabytes. WD HDDs are available in capacities up to 32 TB per drive to support growth without adding drive count. Plan for 30–50% annual growth and always keep a separate backup copy."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 6,
     "text": "NAS",
     "href": "/solutions/network-attached-storage"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 7,
     "text": "enterprise",
     "href": "/solutions/data-center-storage-platform"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 8,
     "text": "What kind of hard drive do I need for a NAS?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 9,
     "text": "should be rated for 24/7 operation and optimized for multi-drive vibration tolerance. drives are purpose-built for NAS, with firmware tuned for multi-bay systems and workloads up to 180 TB/year for . Plan for at least two drives for redundancy and match total capacity to your needs, accounting for the configuration you choose."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 10,
     "text": "NAS drives",
     "href": "/solutions/network-attached-storage/nas-hard-drives"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 11,
     "text": "WD Red Pro",
     "href": "/products/internal-drives/wd-red-pro-sata-hdd"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 12,
     "text": "Red Pro",
     "href": "/products/internal-drives/wd-red-pro-sata-hdd"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 13,
     "text": "RAID",
     "href": "/solutions/raid"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 14,
     "text": "What storage do AI and machine learning workloads need?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 15,
     "text": "AI workloads generate enormous volumes of training data, model checkpoints, and outputs — typically far more than flash storage can economically hold. High-capacity enterprise HDDs form the foundation of AI storage, serving as the data lake for training datasets, model versions, and long-term archives. WD's are engineered for this capacity tier, supporting AI factories, HPC clusters, and hyperscale cloud."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 16,
     "text": "Ultrastar HDDs and platform storage systems",
     "href": "/solutions/data-center"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 17,
     "text": "How should I back up my data?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 18,
     "text": "A complete backup strategy follows the 3-2-1 rule: three copies of your data, on two different types of media, with one copy stored offsite. Most users achieve this with a WD for local backup, paired with a cloud or copy for offsite protection. Built-in backup software handles scheduling and recovery automatically."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 19,
     "text": "external or internal HDD",
     "href": "https://www.westerndigital.com/products/hdd"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 20,
     "text": "NAS",
     "href": "/solutions/network-attached-storage"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 21,
     "text": "How long does a hard drive last?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 22,
     "text": "and -rated hard drives are engineered for high-duty-cycle operation and typically last 5–7 years under continuous workloads, with many lasting longer. , , , and drives are rated for high mean time between failures (MTBF) and carry multi-year limited warranties. Consumer HDDs in lighter-use scenarios typically last 3–5 years. Regular backups and replacement planning matter more than drive age alone."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 23,
     "text": "Enterprise",
     "href": "/solutions/data-center"
    },
    {
