    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 45,
     "text": "What is the difference between NAS and RAID?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 46,
     "text": "is a networked device that lets multiple users share storage across a network. is a method of combining multiple hard drives within one system for performance, redundancy, or both. Most NAS devices use RAID internally — they're complementary, not competing technologies. Both rely on NAS-rated HDDs like for long-term reliability."
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 47,
     "text": "NAS",
     "href": "/solutions/network-attached-storage"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 48,
     "text": "RAID",
     "href": "/solutions/raid"
    },
    {
     "role": "cta",
     "order": 49,
     "text": "WD Red Pro",
     "href": "/products/internal-drives/wd-red-pro-sata-hdd"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 50,
     "text": "Do I need RAID if I have a backup?"
    },
    {
     "role": "body",
     "order": 51,
     "text": "and serve different purposes. RAID protects against drive failure during use — if one drive fails, the system keeps running with no interruption. Backup protects against data loss events like accidental deletion, ransomware, fire, or theft. Most businesses need both, typically using in a RAID array combined with external or offsite backup drives."
    },
